 ## Encryption
 
@@ -218,7 +220,7 @@ Third party actions in the workflows are pinned by commit SHA rather than by tag
 
 Telsiz is designed to hide content from the server, but it does not hide everything, and like any software it cannot be guaranteed to be free of vulnerabilities. The project has not had an independent security audit. The known limits are as follows.
 
-**Metadata the server sees.** The server cannot see message, file, profile or DJ content, but it does see: which user is online when and their status (including the invisible status), usernames, room names and the room list, roles, who writes to which room and direct message conversation, when and with what size, edit and delete events, the sizes of uploaded files, friendship and block relations, typing status (who is typing in which conversation and when), who is in which voice room, their mute and deafen state and whose camera is on, who calls whom in direct messages and when, whether a call is voice or video and how long it lasts, the timing and size of voice signals, which voice rooms have a Telsiz DJ state, the size of the DJ envelope and who updates it and when, if the owner has turned on search by song name who searched for which song name with `/play` (plain text, not logged), session labels derived from the browser's user agent (for example the browser and operating system name), and the IP addresses of connecting devices. Display names, about me, custom status text and profile pictures are encrypted. The frequency name, the frequency introduction and the frequency photo are not encrypted and are public.
+**Metadata the server sees.** The server cannot see message, file, profile or DJ content, but it does see: which user is online when and their status (including the invisible status), if automatic idle is on when a person's sessions went inactive (members see it too once all of their sessions are idle), usernames, room names and the room list, roles, who writes to which room and direct message conversation, when and with what size, edit and delete events, the sizes of uploaded files, friendship and block relations, typing status (who is typing in which conversation and when), who is in which voice room, their mute and deafen state and whose camera is on, who calls whom in direct messages and when, whether a call is voice or video and how long it lasts, the timing and size of voice signals, which voice rooms have a Telsiz DJ state, the size of the DJ envelope and who updates it and when, if the owner has turned on search by song name who searched for which song name with `/play` (plain text, not logged), session labels derived from the browser's user agent (for example the browser and operating system name), and the IP addresses of connecting devices. Display names, about me, custom status text and profile pictures are encrypted. The frequency name, the frequency introduction and the frequency photo are not encrypted and are public.
 
 **Code delivery in the web version.** In the web version the app code comes from the server on every visit. An active attacker who takes over the server, or an intermediary that terminates the HTTPS connection (a reverse proxy or a tunnel provider), could serve modified code and steal keys. This is the known limit of every end-to-end encrypted app that runs in a browser. The desktop app removes this risk because it carries the interface inside itself.
 
